    # c04.s03
    scene bg workshop
    with fade
    "By day Jef taught him the craft."

    "The workshop was the largest room in the house and the only untidy one. There were lamps in pieces, and things like the lamp's heart laid open, and coils of a pale wire that was warm to the touch. Along one wall hung tools. They were small, and of no shape Wrack could have described, and each had been worn by a hand until it shone."

    show p wrack_tunic_attentive as left at head_left
    show p jef_attentive as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "The mariners say tend, mend, make,"
    "Jef said, on the first morning. He was hunting for something under a bench."
    c_jef "Tend is what the holds do. Wipe it, sing to it, don't poke it. I've nothing against that. It's kept the machines alive three thousand years, which is more than poking would have. Mend is what I do. Find out why it's stopped, and stop it stopping. Make."
    "He came up with a dented grey ovoid and blew dust off it."
    c_jef "Make is building a new one from nothing. I can do that too. Slowly. One air-heart takes me four years. There are two thousand air-hearts in the world, and they're all the same age."
    "He set the thing on the bench."
    c_jef "This is a warm-stone. It heats a room. It's the simplest thing they made. Tell me what's wrong with it."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "It was not like learning. It was like being reminded."

    "Jef would open a machine on the bench and begin to explain it, and Wrack's hands would already be moving. The old man watched this, day after day, with an expression that was hard to read. He was a good teacher, and it was plain that he had expected to be needed more than he was."
    "He was impatient with words, and endlessly patient with the work itself. He would show a thing once and then go and sit on a stool with a mug while Wrack did it wrong six times. On the seventh he would say,"
    show p jef_attentive as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "There,"
    "as if he had known all along which try it would be."

    show p wrack_tunic_attentive as left at head_left
    with qd
    c_jef "You've done this before,"
    "he said once."
    c_jef "Not {i}this{/i}. But something. You've stood over drawings. You've told other people where to put things. You think in sections, look, you always start by working out where you'd cut it."
    "He sniffed."
    c_jef "Agravar training. All those straight lines."
    c_wrack "Is that bad?"
    show p jef_grumbling as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "It's different. The Makers didn't think in straight lines. They thought like water. Everything they built wants to flow from somewhere to somewhere, and when it stops, it's silted up, or it's seated wrong, or it's worn through. That's all there is. Clear it, seat it, or replace it."
    "He drank."
    c_jef "You'll be better than me at seeing where to cut. And worse at knowing when not to. Hand me that."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    scene bg kettle_house
    with dissolve
    "In the afternoons, when Jef's hands tired, they sat on the step, and it was then that the old man talked. He did not tell his story. He let pieces of it fall, like a man emptying his pockets."

    "He had been a sick child. That was how he had come to the Lantern. He had had a wasting fever at six years old, and had been carried north on the Dragon by an aunt, and left in a pool in the fourth hall. The healers cured him in a season. Nobody came to fetch him."
    show p wrack_tunic_attentive as left at head_left
    show p jef_tired as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "There was a war on, down in the Anthem Waters,"
    "he said."
    c_jef "People had other things to think of."
    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "So he had stayed. He swept the halls and carried for the healers. And at night, when the patients slept, a small boy with nothing to do wandered through nine halls of Maker glass, and in those days the walls still spoke."

    show p wrack_tunic_attentive as left at head_left
    show p jef_watching as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "Archive walls,"
    c_jef "You've not seen one working. Hardly anyone alive has. You'd put your hand on the glass and it would light, and there'd be pictures. Moving. How a thing was built. Every part, one after another, as slow as you liked."
    "He held his palm up flat, remembering it."
    c_jef "The healers thought it was pretty. I thought it was the only thing in the world. I sat in front of those walls for eleven years. I learned their writing off them. I'd get a tool out of a store nobody'd opened in two thousand years and hold it up to the picture to see which way round it went."
    c_wrack "Where are the walls now?"
    show p jef_tired as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "Dark. They went one at a time. The last one in the Lantern went out when I was about three hundred. I've never found out how to mend them; they never showed me themselves."
    "He turned the mug in his hands."
    c_jef "So it's all in here now."
    "He tapped his forehead."
    c_jef "One old head. I've told the elders for six centuries that it's a stupid place to keep it."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "Of the war, and of Sounding, he said nothing at all. Wrack did not ask."

    # c04.s04
    scene bg well_lip
    with fade
    "In the second week Jef took him down the Well."

    "They went on a deep-runner, which was a needle's stout cousin, blunt and thick-hulled and slow. Jef rode it as Okonta rode hers, without touching anything. The descent took nine hours. The water grew cold, and then colder, and so heavy that Wrack's bones ached and each breath was like lifting a weight."
    "The jellies were left behind. The green weed gave out. There was nothing but the runner's small light on the black wall of the trench, going by, and the star below them, which did not seem to grow."

    "Halfway down something uncoiled from the wall."

    "It was the thing he had felt. An eye the size of a cartwheel regarded them, gold, with a slotted pupil. Behind the first there were others, a whole wall of them, stirring."

    show p wrack_tunic_frightened as left at head_left
    show p jef_attentive as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "Well-wardens,"
    c_jef "Squid. Don't stare, it's rude."
    "He held up a small worn disc of the pale metal, and the arm withdrew."
    c_jef "Nobody put them here. They came on their own, about the time the hospital did, and they've let nobody down since who meant harm. I've no idea how they tell. I asked Maelis to ask the rays, and the rays thought it was funny."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "Then at last the star grew, and came apart into nine."

    scene cg c4_lantern_crown
    with dissolve
    "The Lantern stood on the floor of the Well. It was nine great halls of Maker glass, set in a ring and joined by covered ways, each hall as large as the whole dome of Low Anthem. The glass was the colour of milk and lit from within, so that the hospital stood in the black water like a crown of lamps."
    "And all around it, even here, even in that cold, things grew: white fans, and pale flowers the size of his hand, feeding on the warmth that leaked from the walls."

    scene bg lantern_hall
    with dissolve
    "Inside it was warm and bright and very quiet. In the pools the sick of the world floated, in water that glowed faintly gold. Healers sat at the edge of every pool with their hands in the water. They worked in shifts, Jef said, three to a pool, day and night, so that the intention never lapsed."
    "Wrack saw a child whose hand was growing back; it was translucent still, like a hand made of wax, and she was holding it up to the light and turning it. He saw a man lying with his chest open like a book, and under the water things were being put right in it."
    "He saw an old woman, brought from the far side of the world, whose lungs had been remade. She was breathing in her sleep without effort. Two pools along, in the same hall, the old man from Fallow lay with his daughter's hand on his forehead."

    "The gift was strong here. He could feel the sea leaning close, as a person leans over a cradle."

    show p wrack_tunic_awed as left at head_left
    show p jef_tired as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_wrack "Why here?"
    c_wrack "Why is it stronger?"
    c_jef "Don't know. I'm a mechanic."
    "Jef was walking slowly, nodding to healers, who bowed to him in a way he clearly disliked."
    c_jef "Maelis says it's because it's the oldest water there is. It went down at the Tips before there were people and it's never been anywhere else. Nothing's ever been put in it but care."
    "He stopped at a tall archway."
    c_jef "Here's what I wanted you to see."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    scene bg dark_hall
    with dissolve
    "The hall beyond was dark. The pools were dry. So was the hall after it, and the two after that. Four of the nine."

    show p wrack_tunic_troubled as left at head_left
    show p jef_tired as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "Five hundred years I've been patching this place,"
    "He did not grumble it. He said it quietly, with his hand on the cold glass of the arch."
    c_jef "There's one great heart under the middle of the ring that feeds all nine halls. It's going. I can't get at it without shutting down the five that still work, and if I shut them down, the water in those pools goes dead in an hour, and so do some of the people in them. So I've mended round the edges. And every hundred years or so, another hall goes."
    "He looked at Wrack sidelong."
    c_jef "Unless somebody could hold the water in five halls just as it is. Warm, and still, and cared for. While somebody else had his hands in the works."
    c_wrack "I can't intend. I don't know how."
    c_jef "You don't need to. The healers will go on intending. You only need to keep the water from knowing the heart's stopped."
    "He scratched his chin."
    c_jef "It's a fool's job. I'd not ask if I had another two hundred years."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "It took Wrack six days."

    "He sat in the centre of the ring with his hands in a channel of seawater, and asked the sea, pool by pool, to stay as it was. That was Holding. He had done it at Kettle House with a cupful. Here there were three hundred pools, and a person in each. He did not shout. He was too frightened to shout."
    "He asked, and went on asking, hour after hour, and felt the Well lean in around him and take the weight. Under the floor Jef worked, with a lamp between his teeth, calling up for tools. On the fourth day Wrack went down to help him, still holding, and discovered that he could do both."
    "One part of him kept three hundred pools still, and another lay on its back in a crawlway beside a nine-hundred-year-old man, handing him things."

    scene bg lantern_hall
    with dissolve
    "On the seventh day all nine halls came alight together."

    "The healers stood in the covered ways and wept. A very old one, a woman who had been at the Lantern sixty years, went into the fourth hall and stood in the middle of it, turning round and round."
    show p jef_grumbling as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "Hm,"
    hide right
    with qd
    "and went to see about lunch."

    # c04.s05
    scene bg step_night
    with fade
    "By night the sea taught him the rest. Jef could not help with that."

    show p wrack_tunic_attentive as left at head_left
    show p jef_tired as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "I've no gift,"
    "He was sitting on the step with a cup of something hot, watching. It had become his habit."
    c_jef "Never had. I'm a mechanic. The sea doesn't talk to me; I just fix her plumbing. The callers have the gift, and they can't tell a coupling from a clam. That's how it's always been. Makers' craft in one skin and sea's gift in another, and never the two together."
    "He drank."
    c_jef "Then you."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "It was Maelis who taught him the names of what he was doing. She was a healer, not a caller, but she had sat with callers at the end of their strength for fifty years and knew the road. Listening came first, she said, and he had found that for himself. After it came Moving, the plain push and pull, a current where there had been none."
    "Then Shaping, giving water an edge and a form, a wall, a rope, a bubble of air. Then Holding, which he had learned at the Lantern in the hardest way. Raising came last of all, the lifting of water against its own weight. Most callers lived and died without it."

    show p wrack_tunic_attentive as left at head_left
    show p maelis_composed as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_maelis "You began at the wrong end,"
    c_maelis "You raised a ship before you could listen to a crab. It's as if a child ran before it had seen its own feet. No wonder she knocked you down."
    "She was sitting beside Jef with a rug over her knees."
    c_maelis "Go back to the beginning. Do each one small. Ask for less than you think you can have. You'll be astonished how much she gives a person who doesn't grab."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "So he went back. Night after night he sat at the lip of the Well, under the turning sun, and asked for small things. He asked for a ripple, and then for a current to carry one jelly a little to the left. He made a rope of water and tied it in a knot. He held a bowl of sea upside down in the air until it was easy, and then until it was dull."
    "Every night the sea gave him a little more, as though it had only been waiting for manners. He was ebbed twice, mildly, a day of cold and thirst each time. After the second he began to know the feeling of the edge before he reached it."

    scene cg c4_sphere
    with dissolve
    "At the end of a month a sphere of water a hundred metres across hung in the air over the Well, turning slowly, with a living shoal inside it. The low sun shone through it and laid a moving red light across the ice."

    scene bg step_night
    with dissolve
    show p wrack_tunic_baffled as left at head_left
    show p jef_attentive as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_wrack "Why me?"
    "He set it down as gently as an egg."
    c_jef "Don't know,"
    c_jef "Maelis thinks you do, somewhere under all that."
    show p maelis_composed as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_maelis "I think you had a foot in each world before you had a name,"
    c_maelis "I think that's what the sea likes about you. But I'm guessing. The rays know, and they won't say. They say it's yours to find."

    hide right
    with qd
    "She went in. Jef stayed, turning the cup in his hands."

    show p jef_tired as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "I'll tell you what I do know,"
    c_jef "I'm dying. Don't make that face. I've had nine hundred years and I'm sick of my own cooking. I found a thing in the walls once, when I was young, that keeps a body going. Maker medicine. It's a machine, like everything of theirs. I've mended it as often as it'll stand."
    "He rubbed his chest absently."
    c_jef "When I go, there's nobody. The lamps go, one at a time. The air-hearts. The holds, and the Dragon. No more Threaded, because I'm the only one who can lay the Thread. A couple of million people in the dark, slowly. It might take two hundred years. That's the worst of it. It'd be so slow that nobody would ever decide to do anything."
    show p wrack_tunic_troubled as left at head_left
    with qd
    c_wrack "You could teach someone."
    show p jef_grumbling as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "I've tried. Sixty apprentices in nine centuries. Clever ones. They could learn to tend. A handful could mend a little, with me standing over them. Not one could carry it away. One carried off everything but the hands, and I'd as soon not talk about him. I used to think it was the mariner in them. Too patient. Too respectful of the thing."
    "He nodded at the place in the sky where the sphere had hung."
    show p jef_watching as right at head_right
    with qd
    c_jef "I was looking for a mechanic. I'd have settled for a middling one. I did not expect to get {i}that{/i}."

    hide left
    hide right
    with qd
    "He went in after a while. Through the window Wrack saw him stop behind Maelis's chair and rest his hand on her shoulder. She put her own up to cover it without looking round."

    # c04.s06
    scene bg kettle_interior
    with fade
    "He came to know her slowly. She did not let fall pieces of herself as Jef did. She answered what she was asked, exactly, and then stopped."

    "She had been born at the other end of the world, in the Quiet, and trained as a healer at the Hush, the southern hospital. At twenty she had been sent to gather venom. The great rays lived in those waters, and their sting killed in seconds, and a drop of it rightly used could wake a stopped heart."
    "The healers took it from the spines the rays shed. She had gone to the place where they gathered, and one of them had come and looked at her."

